######## 76. Env files under other names (.dev.env, production.env) go in with --file

$ kagebunshin next
this checkout is in no kagebunshin project
next (you): draft this folder's project from what the checkout and the connected providers say, and apply it
  kagebunshin project draft --apply
waiting on the human; tell them:
  - 2 more files here hold values in plain text that were not imported into kagebunshin: .dev.env, production.env. To import one, say which environment its values are for: I run its line with that as ENV, and you approve it in the kagebunshin app with the store passphrase. Or run it in a terminal yourself: `kagebunshin onboard --file $T/work/site/.dev.env --env ENV`, `kagebunshin onboard --file $T/work/site/production.env --env ENV`. Delete one that is not needed.

-- onboard takes neither by itself, and says so

(human) $ kagebunshin onboard
onboarding $T/work/site: draft new project site
drafting $T/work/site from the checkout and the providers
reading the state of site

site ($T/work/site): drafted from the checkout and the providers, and installed, with 1 open point
  environment dev: 0 of 2 keys set
  needs you:
    - Please run `kagebunshin store set site.dev API_TOKEN SITE_URL` in a terminal; it asks for the store passphrase, then each value. Or open the kagebunshin app (`kagebunshin app`) and say when it is open: I then run it, and you type the values in the app's window.

not imported: 2 more files with values. `onboard` takes one .env file for each app by itself; to import another, run its line with the environment its values are for as ENV:
  kagebunshin onboard --file $T/work/site/.dev.env --env ENV
  kagebunshin onboard --file $T/work/site/production.env --env ENV

Then, in each checkout above that is not done, paste this line into your coding agent:
  Set up kagebunshin here: run `kagebunshin next` and do what it says until it says done.

-- each goes in by its line, with its environment

(human) $ kagebunshin onboard --file $T/work/site/.dev.env --env dev
onboarding $T/work/site: import .dev.env into project site
Passphrase for the local store (to import 1 env file): 
importing $T/work/site/.dev.env into [site.dev]
writing 2 keys from 1 file to the local store
reading the state of site

site ($T/work/site): used the existing project site, whose environment dev reads the local store, and put 2 keys from .dev.env in the local store's [site.dev]: API_TOKEN, SITE_URL
  environment dev: 2 of 2 keys set
  ready: your agent proves it with a run

not imported: 1 more file with values. `onboard` takes one .env file for each app by itself; to import another, run its line with the environment its values are for as ENV:
  kagebunshin onboard --file $T/work/site/production.env --env ENV

Then, in each checkout above that is not done, paste this line into your coding agent:
  Set up kagebunshin here: run `kagebunshin next` and do what it says until it says done.

Delete $T/work/site/.dev.env now that its values are in the store? [y/N] kept $T/work/site/.dev.env; delete it once `kagebunshin run -- COMMAND` works

(human) $ kagebunshin onboard --file production.env --env production
onboarding $T/work/site: import production.env into project site, as its new environment production
Passphrase for the local store (to import 1 env file): 
importing $T/work/site/production.env into [site.production]
writing 2 keys from 1 file to the local store
warning: environment production of project site looks like production (its name has the segment production) and waits for a human decision; mark it sensitive with `kagebunshin mark env site production`, since it is production by its name, or ask the human: open the app (`kagebunshin app`) and either mark site/production sensitive or acknowledge it is not production
reading the state of site

site ($T/work/site): gave project site the environment production, and put 2 keys from production.env in the local store's [site.production]: API_TOKEN, SITE_URL
  environment production: 2 of 2 keys set
  needs your agent: mark site.production sensitive: it is production by its name, so its values are handed out only once the human approves the request in the app

Then, in each checkout above that is not done, paste this line into your coding agent:
  Set up kagebunshin here: run `kagebunshin next` and do what it says until it says done.

Delete $T/work/site/production.env now that its values are in the store? [y/N] kept $T/work/site/production.env; delete it once `kagebunshin run -- COMMAND` works

$ cat $HOME/.config/kagebunshin/projects/site.toml
# Drafted by `kagebunshin project draft`. Check it with
# `kagebunshin project apply FILE --check`.
schema = 1
remotes = ["github.com/acme/site"]
# left out: AWS profile acme-prod: not checked: it looks like production (has the part prod)

[envs.dev]
# open: no secret found for API_TOKEN, SITE_URL
#   kagebunshin store set site.dev API_TOKEN SITE_URL   (human)

[[envs.production.sources]]
provider = "file"

$ kagebunshin run --strict -- ./test.sh
ok   API_TOKEN
ok   SITE_URL

$ kagebunshin next
project site, environment dev: 2 of 2 keys set, proven; an environment named production is left to mark
next (you): mark site.production sensitive: it is production by its name, so its values are handed out only once the human approves the request in the app
  kagebunshin mark env site production

-- a second file for the same environment goes in over the first, and says what it replaced

(human) $ kagebunshin --json onboard --file local.env --env dev --keep-env
onboarding $T/work/site: import local.env into project site
Passphrase for the local store (to import 1 env file): 
importing $T/work/site/local.env into [site.dev]
writing 1 key from 1 file to the local store
reading the state of site
{"deleted":[],"not_imported":[],"paste":"Set up kagebunshin here: run `kagebunshin next` and do what it says until it says done.","projects":[{"did":"used the existing project site, whose environment dev reads the local store, and put 1 key from local.env in the local store's [site.dev]: API_TOKEN; 1 of them replaced a value already there: API_TOKEN","dir":"$T/work/site","done":false,"env":"dev","imported":{"files":["$T/work/site/local.env"],"keys":["API_TOKEN"]},"of":2,"project":"site","set":2,"state":["needs your agent"],"step":{"command":"kagebunshin mark env site production","tell":null,"what":"mark site.production sensitive: it is production by its name, so its values are handed out only once the human approves the request in the app","who":"agent"},"tell":[]}],"skipped":[]}

-- in a repository of several apps, a file outside every app's folder needs its project named

$ kagebunshin next
this repository has 2 apps, each its own project: all done
  apps/web: project mono-web, environment dev: done. 2 of 2 keys set; `./test.sh` ran with them on <day>. They come from file:mono-web.dev (2).
  apps/worker: project mono-worker, environment dev: done. 2 of 2 keys set; `./test.sh` ran with them on <day>. They come from aws_sm:acme/dev/api (1), aws_sm:acme/dev/queue (1).
left for the human, when they like; tell them:
  - $T/work/mono/apps/web/.env still holds values in plain text, and mono-web now gets its secrets from kagebunshin: delete the file once nothing in it is still needed (`rm $T/work/mono/apps/web/.env`).
  - 1 more file here holds values in plain text that were not imported into kagebunshin: staging.env. To import one, say which environment its values are for and which project: I run its line with that as ENV and PROJECT, and you approve it in the kagebunshin app with the store passphrase. Or run it in a terminal yourself: `kagebunshin onboard --file $T/work/mono/staging.env --env ENV --name PROJECT`. Delete one that is not needed.

(human) $ kagebunshin onboard --file staging.env --env staging
kagebunshin: $T/work/mono/staging.env is in the folder of none of the 2 apps of $T/work/mono, so nothing says which project it is for; nothing was written
hint: say which with `--name NAME` after the same command: one of mono-web (apps/web), mono-worker (apps/worker)
[exit 2]

(human) $ kagebunshin onboard --file staging.env --env staging --name mono-web
onboarding $T/work/mono/apps/web: import $T/work/mono/staging.env into project mono-web, as its new environment staging
Passphrase for the local store (to import 1 env file): 
importing $T/work/mono/staging.env into [mono-web.staging]
writing 2 keys from 1 file to the local store
reading the state of mono-web

mono-web ($T/work/mono/apps/web): gave project mono-web the environment staging, and put 2 keys from $T/work/mono/staging.env in the local store's [mono-web.staging]: SESSION_SECRET, PUBLIC_URL
  environment staging: 2 of 2 keys set
  ready: your agent proves it with a run

